 # Approximate Cell Sampling
-
-
 ## Overview
-Approximate Cell Sampling is a method for sampling cell-centered data in tetrahedral volume rendering that estimates the contribution of neighboring tetrahedra without performing a full tree traversal to locate their vertices [S1, unofficial]. It solves the problem of accessing neighbor geometry when vertex data is not stored for non-coarse elements, using volume ratios and face normals to approximate neighbor centroids [S1, unofficial]. It is distinguished from [Simple Cell Sampling](1037-vertex-centered-data-sampling.md) by weighting samples by the inverse distance to the estimated centroids, and from [Vertex-centered Data Sampling](1037-vertex-centered-data-sampling.md) by operating on cell-centered data rather than vertex-centered data [S1, unofficial].
+Approximate Cell Sampling is a method for sampling cell-centered data in tetrahedral volume rendering that estimates the contribution of neighboring tetrahedra without performing a full tree traversal to locate their vertices [S1, unofficial]. It solves the problem of accessing neighbor geometry when vertex data is not stored for non-coarse elements, using volume ratios and face normals to approximate neighbor centroids [S1, unofficial]. It is distinguished from Simple Cell Sampling by weighting samples by the inverse distance to the estimated centroids, and from [Vertex-centered Data Sampling](1037-vertex-centered-data-sampling.md) by operating on cell-centered data rather than vertex-centered data [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## How it works
 **Inputs:**
-- `point`: The 3D sample point in world space.
-- `V`: An array of 4 vertices `[t, l, r, b]`, where each vertex is a 3D coordinate tuple.
-- `tetID`: The ID of the current tetrahedron.
-- `coarseTetID`: The ID of the coarse tetrahedron containing the current element.
-- `neighbors`: An array of neighbor IDs, where neighbors for `tetID` are located at `neighbors[4 * tetID + i]` for `i` in `0..3`.
-- `cellData`: An array of scalar values associated with each tetrahedron.
